{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Longer natural‐language prompts reduce the success rate of molecular edits, as measured by the proportion of examples in train.parquet vs train_neg.parquet across prompt‐length deciles.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between prompt length and edit success rate.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet and concatenate into a single DataFrame with a binary label “success” (1 for train, 0 for train_neg).\n2. Compute prompt_length = number of words (or tokens) in the prompt column.\n3. Bin examples into deciles (10 bins) based on prompt_length.\n4. For each decile, compute the success rate = (# successful edits) / (total edits).\n5. Perform a Cochran–Armitage trend test or logistic regression of success ~ prompt_length decile to assess monotonic trend.\n6. Plot success rate vs. prompt_length decile with 95% confidence intervals.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Table of prompt_length deciles vs. success rate and counts. (b) Trend test statistic and p‐value. (c) Line plot of success rate across deciles with confidence bands."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits (train.parquet), replace operations produce a higher average target_delta than insert or delete operations, controlling for task_id.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the effectiveness of action_type on target_delta within successful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to non‐null target_delta.\n2. For each task_id, compute mean target_delta for each action_type (replace, insert, delete).\n3. Perform a two‐way ANOVA with factors task_id and action_type on target_delta.\n4. If interaction or main effects are significant, run post‐hoc pairwise comparisons (Tukey’s HSD) between action_types within each task.\n5. Visualize results: for each action_type, plot mean±SEM target_delta aggregated across tasks.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) ANOVA table (F‐statistics, p‐values) for action_type and task_id factors. (b) Post‐hoc comparison results. (c) Bar chart of mean target_delta by action_type with error bars."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Retaining the Murcko scaffold increases the probability of a successful edit, after adjusting for task_id.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the effect of murcko_scaffold_retained on edit success.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets, label success=1 for train.parquet and success=0 for train_neg.parquet.\n2. Remove rows with missing murcko_scaffold_retained.\n3. Encode murcko_scaffold_retained as binary (1/0) and task_id as categorical.\n4. Fit a logistic regression: success ~ murcko_scaffold_retained + C(task_id).\n5. Extract odds ratio for murcko_scaffold_retained, 95% CI, and p‐value.\n6. Validate model calibration (e.g., calibration curve) and compute AUC.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Logistic regression coefficients with odds ratios and CIs. (b) Model performance metrics (AUC, calibration plot). (c) Interpretation of scaffold retention effect."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Shorter edit descriptions (fewer characters in the edit column) correlate with larger improvements in target_delta among successful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between edit description complexity and optimization success magnitude.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to non‐null target_delta.\n2. Compute edit_length = number of characters (or tokens) in the edit string.\n3. Calculate Pearson or Spearman correlation between edit_length and target_delta.\n4. Fit a linear regression target_delta ~ edit_length + C(task_id) to adjust for task effects.\n5. Plot a scatter plot with a regression line of target_delta vs. edit_length, colored by task cluster or aggregated trend.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficient and p‐value. (b) Regression summary (coefficients, R^2). (c) Scatter plot with regression overlay."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In QED‐optimization tasks (task_id=103), successful edits exhibit a negative correlation between QED_delta and SA_delta, indicating a trade‐off between drug‐likeness improvement and synthetic accessibility.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for a QED vs. SA trade‐off in successful QED‐focused edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to task_id==103 and non‐null QED_delta and SA_delta.\n2. Compute Pearson or Spearman correlation between QED_delta and SA_delta.\n3. Test significance of correlation.\n4. Fit a simple linear model SA_delta ~ QED_delta to estimate slope.\n5. Visualize with a scatter plot and regression line; annotate correlation coefficient.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficient and p‐value. (b) Regression slope with CI. (c) Scatter plot of SA_delta vs. QED_delta for task_id=103."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For water‐solubility optimization (task_id=101), edits that decrease molecular weight (negative MW_delta) achieve greater SA_delta improvements among successful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Examine the relationship between MW_delta direction and SA_delta change in successful solubility edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet, filter to task_id==101 and non‐null MW_delta, SA_delta.\n2. Split examples into two groups: MW_delta < 0 and MW_delta >= 0.\n3. Compare mean SA_delta between the two groups using an unpaired t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test.\n4. Compute effect size (Cohen’s d) for SA_delta difference.\n5. Plot boxplots of SA_delta for the two MW_delta groups.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Group means and test statistic/p‐value. (b) Effect size. (c) Boxplot of SA_delta by MW_delta sign."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"A logistic regression model using MW_delta, SA_delta, QED_delta, action_type, murcko_scaffold_retained, and prompt_length predicts edit success with AUC>0.75.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Build and evaluate a multivariate classifier to predict edit success.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets, label success as before, and drop rows with any missing features.\n2. Derive prompt_length and encode action_type and murcko_scaffold_retained as dummy variables.\n3. Split data into train/test (e.g., 80/20 stratified by success).\n4. Standardize numeric features and fit a logistic regression (with L2 regularization) on the training set.\n5. Evaluate on the test set: compute ROC curve, AUC, accuracy, precision, recall.\n6. Perform 5‐fold cross‐validation to estimate generalization performance.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Test set AUC, accuracy, precision, recall. (b) ROC curve. (c) Cross‐validation mean AUC and standard deviation."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tasks with longer average prompt lengths have lower overall success rates, indicating greater task complexity.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Identify whether prompt verbosity per task predicts difficulty (lower success rate).",  
      "steps":"1. For each dataset, compute prompt_length.\n2. For each task_id, compute mean prompt_length and success rate = (# successes)/(# successes + # failures).\n3. Across the 14 tasks, compute Pearson or Spearman correlation between mean prompt_length and success rate.\n4. Fit a linear regression success_rate ~ mean_prompt_length.\n5. Visualize with scatter plot of success rate vs. mean prompt_length and regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficient and p‐value. (b) Regression summary. (c) Scatter plot with fitted line."  
    }  
  }  
]}
